Below is a **complete, ready‑to‑run Java implementation** of a Red‑Black Tree that supports the two core operations you need:

* **Insertion** (including the usual RB‑tree fix‑up)
* **Deletion** (including the more involved fix‑up)

The code is organized into a single file (`RedBlackTree.java`) so you can compile and run it directly.  
I’ve added extensive comments so you can see **why** each step is performed – this will also help you understand the algorithm for your course.

---

## `RedBlackTree.java`

```java
/**
 * Red‑Black Tree implementation in Java.
 *
 * This file contains:
 *   - The Node class (inner class) representing a tree node.
 *   - The RedBlackTree class with:
 *       • insert(key)      – adds a key while preserving Red‑Black properties.
 *       • delete(key)      – removes a key while preserving Red‑Black properties.
 *       • contains(key)    – helper to check existence (used by delete).
 *       • inorder()        – helper to print the tree (for testing).
 *
 * The tree follows the classic textbook definition:
 *   1. Every node is either RED or BLACK.
 *   2. The root is BLACK.
 *   3. All leaves (NIL) are BLACK.
 *   4. If a node is RED, both its children are BLACK.
 *   5. For each node, all simple paths from the node to descendant leaves contain the same
 *      number of BLACK nodes.
 *
 * Compilation & execution (command line):
 *   $ javac RedBlackTree.java
 *   $ java RedBlackTree
 *
 * The main method contains a short demo that inserts a few keys, deletes one,
 * and prints the resulting inorder traversal.
 *
 * Feel free to modify the demo or add your own tests.
 */

public class RedBlackTree {

    // ---------- Node inner class ----------
    private static final boolean RED   = true;
    private static final boolean BLACK = false;

    private class Node {
        int key;
        boolean color;            // RED or BLACK
        Node left, right, parent;

        Node(int key, boolean color) {
            this.key = key;
            this.color = color;
            left = right = parent = NIL;   // NIL is a shared sentinel node
        }
    }

    // NIL is a shared sentinel representing all external (null) children.
    private final Node NIL = new Node(Integer.MIN_VALUE, BLACK);
    private Node root = NIL;   // initially the tree is empty

    // ======================  Rotations  ======================

    /** Left rotate x. */
    private void leftRotate(Node x) {
        Node y = x.right;
        x.right = y.left;
        if (y.left != NIL) {
            y.left.parent = x;
        }
        y.parent = x.parent;
        if (x.parent == NIL) {
            root = y;
        } else if (x == x.parent.left) {
            x.parent.left = y;
        } else {
            x.parent.right = y;
        }
        y.left = x;
        x.parent = y;
    }

    /** Right rotate y. */
    private void rightRotate(Node y) {
        Node x = y.left;
        y.left = x.right;
        if (x.right != NIL) {
            x.right.parent = y;
        }
        x.parent = y.parent;
        if (y.parent == NIL) {
            root = x;
        } else if (y == y.parent.right) {
            y.parent.right = x;
        } else {
            y.parent.left = x;
        }
        x.right = y;
        y.parent = x;
    }

    // ======================  Insertion  ======================

    /** Public method to insert a new key into the tree. */
    public void insert(int key) {
        Node z = new Node(key, RED);
        Node y = NIL;
        Node x = root;

        // Find the insertion point (standard BST insert)
        while (x != NIL) {
            y = x;
            if (z.key < x.key) {
                x = x.left;
            } else {
                x = x.right;
            }
        }
        z.parent = y;
        if (y == NIL) {
            root = z;
        } else if (z.key < y.key) {
            y.left = z;
        } else {
            y.right = z;
        }

        // Insert fix-up to restore RB properties
        insertFixup(z);
    }

    /** Fix‑up procedure for insertion. */
    private void insertFixup(Node z) {
        while (z.parent.color == RED) {
            if (z.parent == z.parent.parent.left) {
                Node y = z.parent.parent.right;           // uncle
                if (y.color == RED) {                    // case 1
                    z.parent.color = BLACK;
                    y.color = BLACK;
                    z.parent.parent.color = RED;
                    z = z.parent.parent;
                } else {
                    if (z == z.parent.right) {           // case 2
                        z = z.parent;
                        leftRotate(z);
                    }
                    // case 3
                    z.parent.color = BLACK;
                    z.parent.parent.color = RED;
                    rightRotate(z.parent.parent);
                }
            } else { // symmetric case (z.parent is a right child)
                Node y = z.parent.parent.left;            // uncle
                if (y.color == RED) {                     // case 1
                    z.parent.color = BLACK;
                    y.color = BLACK;
                    z.parent.parent.color = RED;
                    z = z.parent.parent;
                } else {
                    if (z == z.parent.left) {            // case 2
                        z = z.parent;
                        rightRotate(z);
                    }
                    // case 3
                    z.parent.color = BLACK;
                    z.parent.parent.color = RED;
                    leftRotate(z.parent.parent);
                }
            }
        }
        root.color = BLACK; // always keep the root black
    }

    // ======================  Deletion  ======================

    /** Public method to delete a key from the tree. */
    public void delete(int key) {
        Node z = findNode(key);
        if (z == NIL) {
            System.out.println("Key " + key + " not found in the tree.");
            return;
        }
        deleteNode(z);
    }

    /** Finds the node with the given key, returns NIL if not present. */
    private Node findNode(int key) {
        Node cur = root;
        while (cur != NIL) {
            if (key == cur.key) {
                return cur;
            } else if (key < cur.key) {
                cur = cur.left;
            } else {
                cur = cur.right;
            }
        }
        return NIL;
    }

    /** Actual deletion logic (standard RB‑tree delete). */
    private void deleteNode(Node z) {
        Node y = z;
        boolean yOriginalColor = y.color;
        Node x;

        if (z.left == NIL) {                     // case: only right child (or none)
            x = z.right;
            transplant(z, z.right);
        } else if (z.right == NIL) {             // case: only left child
            x = z.left;
            transplant(z, z.left);
        } else {                                // case: two children
            y = minimum(z.right);                // successor
            yOriginalColor = y.color;
            x = y.right;
            if (y.parent == z) {
                x.parent = y;
            } else {
                transplant(y, y.right);
                y.right = z.right;
                y.right.parent = y;
            }
            transplant(z, y);
            y.left = z.left;
            y.left.parent = y;
            y.color = z.color;
        }

        // If we removed a BLACK node, we need to fix up the tree
        if (yOriginalColor == BLACK) {
            deleteFixup(x);
        }
    }

    /** Replace the subtree rooted at u with the subtree rooted at v. */
    private void transplant(Node u, Node v) {
        if (u.parent == NIL) {
            root = v;
        } else if (u == u.parent.left) {
            u.parent.left = v;
        } else {
            u.parent.right = v;
        }
        v.parent = u.parent;
    }

    /** Returns the node with the minimum key in the subtree rooted at x. */
    private Node minimum(Node x) {
        while (x.left != NIL) {
            x = x.left;
        }
        return x;
    }

    /** Fix‑up procedure for deletion. */
    private void deleteFixup(Node x) {
        while (x != root && x.color == BLACK) {
            if (x == x.parent.left) {
                Node w = x.parent.right;               // sibling
                if (w.color == RED) {                  // case 1
                    w.color = BLACK;
                    x.parent.color = RED;
                    leftRotate(x.parent);
                    w = x.parent.right;
                }
                if (w.left.color == BLACK && w.right.color == BLACK) { // case 2
                    w.color = RED;
                    x = x.parent;
                } else {
                    if (w.right.color == BLACK) {       // case 3
                        w.left.color = BLACK;
                        w.color = RED;
                        rightRotate(w);
                        w = x.parent.right;
                    }
                    // case 4
                    w.color = x.parent.color;
                    x.parent.color = BLACK;
                    w.right.color = BLACK;
                    leftRotate(x.parent);
                    x = root;
                }
            } else { // symmetric case (x is a right child)
                Node w = x.parent.left;                 // sibling
                if (w.color == RED) {                  // case 1
                    w.color = BLACK;
                    x.parent.color = RED;
                    rightRotate(x.parent);
                    w = x.parent.left;
                }
                if (w.right.color == BLACK && w.left.color == BLACK) { // case 2
                    w.color = RED;
                    x = x.parent;
                } else {
                    if (w.left.color == BLACK) {       // case 3
                        w.right.color = BLACK;
                        w.color = RED;
                        leftRotate(w);
                        w = x.parent.left;
                    }
                    // case 4
                    w.color = x.parent.color;
                    x.parent.color = BLACK;
                    w.left.color = BLACK;
                    rightRotate(x.parent);
                    x = root;
                }
            }
        }
        x.color = BLACK; // always make the root black at the end
    }

    // ======================  Utility methods  ======================

    /** In‑order traversal (for testing / debugging). */
    public void inorder() {
        inorderRec(root);
        System.out.println();
    }

    private void inorderRec(Node node) {
        if (node != NIL) {
            inorderRec(node.left);
            System.out.print(node.key + "(" + (node.color == RED ? "R" : "B") + ") ");
            inorderRec(node.right);
        }
    }

    /** Quick check whether a key exists in the tree. */
    public boolean contains(int key) {
        return findNode(key) != NIL;
    }

    // ======================  Demo / Test harness  ======================

    public static void main(String[] args) {
        RedBlackTree rbt = new RedBlackTree();

        int[] keys = {7, 3, 18, 10, 22, 8, 11, 26, 2, 5, 13, 16, 19};
        System.out.println("Inserting keys: ");
        for (int k : keys) {
            System.out.print(k + " ");
            rbt.insert(k);
        }
        System.out.println("\nInorder after insertions:");
        rbt.inorder();

        // Delete a key that exists
        int delKey = 18;
        System.out.println("\nDeleting key: " + delKey);
        rbt.delete(delKey);
        System.out.println("Inorder after deletion:");
        rbt.inorder();

        // Try to delete a key that does not exist
        rbt.delete(99);

        // Verify contains
        System.out.println("Contains 10? " + rbt.contains(10));
        System.out.println("Contains 18? " + rbt.contains(18));
    }
}
```

---

### How the Code Works (Brief Overview)

| Step | What Happens | Why It Matters |
|------|--------------|----------------|
| **Node structure** | Each node stores `key`, `color` (RED/BLACK), and pointers to left, right, and parent. The sentinel `NIL` simplifies boundary conditions. | Guarantees the tree can represent “external” leaves uniformly. |
| **Insertion** | 1. BST‑style placement.<br>2. Color new node RED.<br>3. `insertFixup` restores the 5 RB‑tree properties via rotations & recolorings. | Keeps the tree balanced (height ≤ 2 log₂(n+1)). |
| **Deletion** | 1. Standard BST delete (handle cases of 0, 1, or 2 children).<br>2. If the removed/deleted node was BLACK, `deleteFixup` re‑balances the tree. | Guarantees that black‑height is consistent along all paths. |
| **Rotations** | `leftRotate` and `rightRotate` rearrange pointers; they are the building blocks for fixing violations. | Provide the O(1) structural changes needed during fix‑ups. |
| **Fix‑up loops** | Both `insertFixup` and `deleteFixup` walk up the tree, applying a handful of cases that each finish with a rotation or recolor. | Ensure all RB‑properties are restored after the structural change. |

---

### Running the Program

1. **Save** the code above into a file named `RedBlackTree.java`.  
2. **Compile** it with your Java compiler (Java 8+ is fine):

   ```bash
   javac RedBlackTree.java
   ```

3. **Run** the demo:

   ```bash
   java RedBlackTree
   ```

   You’ll see the inorder traversal after inserting the sample keys and again after deleting `18`. The tree stays valid (you can add more checks or visual debugging if you wish).

---

### Extending the Code

If you later need extra functionality (e.g., iterators, range queries, or a graphical visualizer), you can add new public methods that traverse the tree while respecting the RB‑tree invariants. The core methods (`insert`, `delete`, `contains`, `inorder`) already give you a solid foundation.

