Here’s a self‑contained, robust Java method that returns the **second‑largest distinct** integer in an array.  
It safely handles the typical edge‑cases (null / empty array, a single element, all‑equal values) and works for any integer values (positive, negative, −2³¹, etc.).  

```java
import java.util.*;

public class SecondLargest {

    /**
     * Returns the second‑largest distinct number in the array.
     * If the array is null, has fewer than two elements, or all values are equal,
     * there is no second distinct value – the method returns -1.
     * (You can change the sentinel to Integer.MIN_VALUE or null if your test
     *  expects a different “no‑result” value.)
     */
    public static int secondLargest(int[] arr) {
        // ---- edge‑case handling -------------------------------------------------
        if (arr == null || arr.length < 2) {
            return -1;                     // not enough elements
        }

        // ---- keep only distinct values (TreeSet = sorted, no duplicates) ----------
        Set<Integer> distinct = new TreeSet<>();
        for (int num : arr) {
            distinct.add(num);
        }

        // ---- if less than two distinct values, no second largest -----------------
        if (distinct.size() < 2) {
            return -1;
        }

        // ---- find the two largest distinct numbers --------------------------------
        int largest = Integer.MIN_VALUE;
        int second  = Integer.MIN_VALUE;
        for (int num : distinct) {
            if (num > largest) {
                second  = largest;   // previous largest becomes second‑largest
                largest = num;       // new largest
            }
        }
        return second;   // second now holds the second‑largest distinct value
    }

    // -----------------------------------------------------------------------
    // Optional test harness – you can delete this part before submitting.
    public static void main(String[] args) {
        int[] a1 = {5, 1, 3, 2, 5, 4};
        System.out.println(secondLargest(a1)); // 4

        int[] a2 = {5, 5, 5};
        System.out.println(secondLargest(a2)); // -1 (no second distinct)

        int[] a3 = {};
        System.out.println(secondLargest(a3)); // -1

        int[] a4 = {42};
        System.out.println(secondLargest(a4)); // -1

        int[] a5 = {2, 1};
        System.out.println(secondLargest(a5)); // 1

        int[] a6 = {-5, -2, -10, -2};
        System.out.println(secondLargest(a6)); // -5
    }
}
```

